1
00:00:01,318 --> 00:00:04,639
The crickets.

2
00:00:05,079 --> 00:00:07,639
[Music]

3
00:00:07,639 --> 00:00:11,239
The crickets sang in the grasses.

4
00:00:11,239 --> 00:00:18,760
They sang the song of summer's ending. A sad, monotonous song. Summer is over and

5
00:00:18,760 --> 00:00:27,559
gone. They sang. Over and gone. over and gone. Summer is dying, dying.

6
00:00:27,559 --> 00:00:35,239
The crickets felt it was their duty to warn everybody that summertime cannot last forever.

7
00:00:35,239 --> 00:00:40,520
Even on the most beautiful days in the whole year, the days when summer is

8
00:00:40,520 --> 00:00:46,600
changing into fall, the crickets spread the rumor of sadness and change.

9
00:00:46,600 --> 00:00:51,398
Everybody heard the song of the crickets. Avery and Fern Arable heard it

10
00:00:51,398 --> 00:00:58,439
as they walked the dusty road. They knew that school would soon begin again.

11
00:00:58,439 --> 00:01:02,680
The young geese heard it and knew that they would never be little golings

12
00:01:02,680 --> 00:01:08,760
again. Charlotte heard it and knew that she hadn't much time left. Mrs.

13
00:01:08,760 --> 00:01:16,600
Zuckerman at work in the kitchen heard the crickets and a sadness came over her too. Another

14
00:01:16,600 --> 00:01:23,000
summer gone," she sighed. Lurvy at work building a crate for Wilbur, heard the

15
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:28,280
song and knew it was time to dig potatoes.

16
00:01:28,280 --> 00:01:35,159
"Summer is over and gone," repeated the crickets. "How many nights till frost,"

17
00:01:35,159 --> 00:01:38,520
sang the crickets. "Goodbye, summer.

18
00:01:38,520 --> 00:01:40,840
Goodbye. Goodbye."

19
00:01:40,840 --> 00:01:46,120
The sheep heard the crickets and they felt so uneasy they broke a hole in the

20
00:01:46,120 --> 00:01:52,040
pasture fence and wandered up into the field across the road.

21
00:01:52,040 --> 00:01:56,439
The gander discovered the hole and led his family through. And they walked to

22
00:01:56,439 --> 00:02:01,318
the orchard and ate the apples that were lying on the ground. A little maple tree

23
00:02:01,318 --> 00:02:07,959
in the swamp heard the cricket song and turned bright red with anxiety.

24
00:02:07,959 --> 00:02:14,120
Wilbur was now the center of attraction on the farm. Good food and regular hours

25
00:02:14,120 --> 00:02:21,398
were showing results. Wilbur was a pig any man would be proud of. One day more

26
00:02:21,398 --> 00:02:26,680
than a hundred people came to stand at his yard and admire him. Charlotte had

27
00:02:26,680 --> 00:02:35,398
written the word radiant, and Wilbur really looked radiant as he stood in the golden sunlight.

28
00:02:35,398 --> 00:02:40,200
Ever since the spider had befriended him, he had done his best to live up to

29
00:02:40,200 --> 00:02:47,318
his reputation. When Charlotte's Web said some pig, Wilbur had tried hard to

30
00:02:47,318 --> 00:02:54,600
look like some pig. When Charlotte's Web said terrific, Wilbur had tried to look

31
00:02:54,600 --> 00:03:04,439
terrific. And now that the web said radiant, he did everything possible to make himself glow.

32
00:03:04,439 --> 00:03:10,120
It is not easy to look radiant, but Wilbur threw himself into it with a

33
00:03:10,120 --> 00:03:16,760
will. He would turn his head slightly and blink his long eyelashes. Then he

34
00:03:16,760 --> 00:03:21,639
would breathe deeply, and when his audience grew bored, he would spring

35
00:03:21,639 --> 00:03:27,239
into the air and do a backflip with a half twist. At this, the crowd would

36
00:03:27,239 --> 00:03:31,559
yell and cheer. "How's that for a pig?"

37
00:03:31,559 --> 00:03:39,879
Mr. Zuckerman would ask, well pleased with himself. That pig is radiant.

38
00:03:39,879 --> 00:03:44,439
Some of Wilbur's friends in the barn worried for fear all this attention

39
00:03:44,439 --> 00:03:51,559
would go to his head and make him stuck up, but it never did. Wilbur was modest.

40
00:03:51,559 --> 00:03:57,879
Fame did not spoil him. He still worried some about the future, as he could

41
00:03:57,879 --> 00:04:03,398
hardly believe that a mere spider would be able to save his life. Sometimes at

42
00:04:03,398 --> 00:04:07,799
night he would have a bad dream. He would dream that men were coming to get

43
00:04:07,799 --> 00:04:13,478
him with knives and guns, but that was only a dream.

44
00:04:13,478 --> 00:04:18,520
In the daytime, Wilbur usually felt happy and confident.

45
00:04:18,520 --> 00:04:24,280
No pig ever had truer friends, and he realized that friendship is one of the

46
00:04:24,280 --> 00:04:27,799
most satisfying things in the world.

47
00:04:27,799 --> 00:04:33,000
Even the song of the crickets did not make Wilbur too sad. He knew it was

48
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:38,200
almost time for the county fair, and he was looking forward to the trip. If he

49
00:04:38,200 --> 00:04:43,639
could distinguish himself at the fair, and maybe win some prize money, he was

50
00:04:43,639 --> 00:04:48,120
sure Zuckerman would let him live.

51
00:04:48,120 --> 00:04:54,920
Charlotte had worries of her own, but she kept quiet about them. One morning,

52
00:04:54,920 --> 00:04:59,959
Wilbur asked her about the fair. "You're going with me, aren't you, Charlotte?"

53
00:04:59,959 --> 00:05:07,318
he said. "Well, I don't know," replied Charlotte. "The fair comes at a bad time

54
00:05:07,318 --> 00:05:15,239
for me. I shall find it inconvenient to leave home, even for a few days." "Why?"

55
00:05:15,239 --> 00:05:23,799
asked Wilbur. "Oh, I just don't feel like leaving my web. Too much going on around here."

56
00:05:23,799 --> 00:05:30,359
"Please come with me," begged Wilbur. "I need you, Charlotte. I can't stand going

57
00:05:30,359 --> 00:05:37,079
to the fair without you. You've just got to come." "No," said Charlotte. "I

58
00:05:37,079 --> 00:05:41,879
believe I'd better stay home and see if I can't get some work done." "What kind

59
00:05:41,879 --> 00:05:48,600
of work?" asked Wilbur. Egg laying. It's time I made an egg sack and filled it

60
00:05:48,600 --> 00:05:56,439
with eggs. I didn't know you could lay eggs, said Wilbur in amazement.

61
00:05:56,439 --> 00:06:01,478
Oh, sure, said the spider. I'm versatile.

62
00:06:01,478 --> 00:06:05,879
What does versatile mean? Full of eggs?

63
00:06:05,879 --> 00:06:08,040
asked Wilbur.

64
00:06:08,040 --> 00:06:14,120
Certainly not, said Charlotte. Versatile means I can turn with ease from one

65
00:06:14,120 --> 00:06:20,120
thing to another. It means I don't have to limit my activities to spinning and

66
00:06:20,120 --> 00:06:25,719
trapping and stunts like that. Why don't you come with me to the fairgrounds and

67
00:06:25,719 --> 00:06:32,280
lay your eggs there? pleaded Wilbur. It would be wonderful fun.

68
00:06:32,280 --> 00:06:38,920
Charlotte gave her web a twitch and moodily watched it sway. I'm afraid not,

69
00:06:38,920 --> 00:06:45,000
she said. You don't know the first thing about egg laying, Wilbur. I can't

70
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:50,600
arrange my family duties to suit the management of the county fair. When I

71
00:06:50,600 --> 00:06:57,239
get ready to lay eggs, I have to lay eggs, fair or no fair. However, I don't

72
00:06:57,239 --> 00:07:03,559
want you to worry about it. You might lose weight. We'll leave it this way.

73
00:07:03,559 --> 00:07:08,520
I'll come to the fair if I possibly can.

74
00:07:08,520 --> 00:07:14,840
Oh, good," said Wilbur. "I knew you wouldn't forsake me just when I needed

75
00:07:14,840 --> 00:07:22,760
you most." All that day, Wilbur stayed inside, taking life easy in the straw.

76
00:07:22,760 --> 00:07:25,799
Charlotte rested and ate a grasshopper.

77
00:07:25,799 --> 00:07:31,079
She knew that she couldn't help Wilbur much longer. In a few days, she would

78
00:07:31,079 --> 00:07:40,478
have to drop everything and build the beautiful little sack that would hold her eggs.

79
00:07:41,079 --> 00:07:42,000
Chapter 16.
